{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And you will seek, etc., with all your heart, etc., in your distress, etc.</b> These two verses that come [together] require explanation. Know that there are two levels of repentance: there is repentance that Israel do on their own, when they recognize their obligation to the Creator and subdue their evil inclination; and there is repentance that they do by means of sufferings, which the enemy brings upon them, and they cry out from the pain of the heart. And corresponding to these two levels Moses spoke to all Israel. Corresponding to one who does repentance even though he has not heard the voice of a taskmaster, he said: <b>And you will seek from there HaShem your Elohim</b> — that the hearts will be aroused to the love of their Elohim — <b>and you will find</b>. And even though you have grave transgressions, as stated nearby: ״וַעֲבַדְתֶּם שָׁם אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים״ etc., none the less you are exempt from [further] sufferings, and the repentance suffices for you. And the reason — <b>for you will seek Him with all your heart</b>: such repentance is complete repentance, with all the heart and with all the soul." "<b>And corresponding to</b> repentance that comes from the side of sufferings, he said: <b>in your distress and you will find Him</b> etc. <b>and you will return</b> etc. — meaning, on account of the sufferings that your enemy has caused you. And here he did not mention ״with all your heart״ etc., because in the midst of the sufferings he is returning. And what is lacking in those who return because of sufferings, in the aspect of the level of the repentance, that is made up for them in the aspect of the sufferings themselves that they underwent, for sufferings purge the transgressions and purify the soul. And what is lacking in the first group, on the side that sufferings did not befall them, that is made up for them in the aspect of the merit of the repentance. And accordingly the statement ״in your distress״ follows on from what precedes it, and its measurement is: <b>And you will seek</b> etc., to the end [of the phrase], <b>and you will find</b>, and the reason: <b>for you will seek Him</b> etc.; <b>or in your distress</b> etc., <b>and you will return</b> — even if it is not with all your heart etc. And the verse gave a reason for HaShem's accepting complete repentance alone without sufferings, or incomplete repentance by means of sufferings: ״כִּי אֵל רַחוּם ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״." "<b>Further</b>, the verses may be explained in this manner: <b>And you will seek from there</b> — meaning, and when you are among the nations there, you will seek Him and pray to Him for the things you need to sustain your lives, and it is not speaking of seeking the redemption. <b>And you will find</b> — that it will be found for you, for if not, they would perish from the world (בַּר מִנַּן). And it spoke in the singular, to say that it will not be found for all of them when they call upon Him in the land of the nations, but only for select individuals (יְחִידֵי סְגֻלָּה). And the reason — <b>for you will seek Him</b> etc. — meaning, one who seeks HaShem requires great exertion, and perhaps select individuals of the nation will attain the request in this manner." "And His saying <b>in your distress</b> — this verse speaks of the promise of the redemption, for ״in the distress of the people of Israel,״ which is the time of the exile, He still has [the promise] that all these things that are stated nearby will befall them — namely, destruction from the land, dispersion among the nations, and the diminution so that those who remain will be few in number; this will be at the end of days, and they are the birth pangs of Mashiach. And they will not have all the troubles continuously over long periods, but only at the end of days. And He promises them that HaShem will then stir their hearts to return in repentance, and this is His saying <b>and you will return to HaShem</b>. And He gave a reason for this promise: ״כִּי אֵל רַחוּם ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ לֹא יַרְפְּךָ״ etc. — and He will strengthen your heart to return etc." "<b>Or perhaps</b> this is the argument of Michael before HaShem that is brought in Zohar Ḥadash (p. 30), for behold the end of days has come and all these things have befallen them — so why does HaShem not stir their hearts to return? And I have explained this teaching in another place. And our eyes are turned to HaShem, watching, to open the treasure of a spirit of purity, to open blind eyes, and to incline the hearts of the people of HaShem to recognize the pleasantness of the sweetness of His intimacy:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:25.162208890Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}